<|start|>assistant to=user<|message|>Everybody called her Aspirin -- she always looked like she was aspiring to become a special state of being, and just looking at her made your headache go away.

She wasn't born with the name. It was given to her at sixteen, in the back room of the pharmacy on Main Street where she worked after school. Mrs. Kowalski, who owned the place and had run out of both ibuprofen and patience, had caught Aspirin — then still just Lena — staring at the ceiling tiles for twenty minutes straight.

"You're daydreaming again," Mrs. Kowalski said, not unkindly. "You gonna aspirate on your own thoughts?"

Lena had laughed, a small surprised sound, and said, "I was just thinking about how nice it would be to be the kind of person people come to when they're hurting."

Mrs. Kowalski had pointed to the bottle on the shelf. "Well, honey, that's what this is for. You want a job? Be Aspirin."

And so it stuck. Lena kept the name even after she left the pharmacy, even after she moved to the city for art school, even after she got married and divorced and opened a tiny studio above a laundromat. People who knew her as a child still called her Aspirin, and new people learned it quickly because she wore it so easily.

She was tall and thin in the way that made people think of a line drawn carefully in pencil. She wore soft, unpatterned clothes in colors that didn't compete. She listened with her whole body, leaning in just slightly, and she had a habit of touching the back of her hand to a person's forehead when they complained of a headache, as if checking a temperature. She never offered advice unless asked twice.

The special state of being she was always aspiring to, it turned out, was not fame or beauty or even peace. It was usefulness. She wanted to be the person who could be present in a room without demanding anything from it. She collected small, quiet skills the way other people collected souvenirs: how to make tea that was exactly the right temperature, how to fold a fitted sheet so it didn't fight back, how to sit with someone crying without trying to fix it.

When her mother got sick, Aspirin moved back home for three months and kept a log of medications, appointments, and the exact time her mother preferred her soup. When her best friend's marriage fell apart, she showed up with a duffel bag and stayed for a week, doing dishes and walking the dog. When the laundromat downstairs flooded, she was the one who called the landlord, mopped the floor, and brought donuts to the neighbors whose dryers were out of commission.

People said she was saintly. She wasn't. She got angry, she was stubborn, she once threw a mug at the wall because the landlord kept ignoring her. She also had a terrible sense of direction and a deep, private fear that she was boring.

The joke about her name was that she made headaches go away. It was true, in a way. She had this way of lowering the temperature of a room. Not by being cool, but by being steady. You could tell her about your promotion and your panic attack in the same sentence and she would nod at both with equal attention.

She never married again. She said she had enough people to take care of. At forty-two she adopted a rescue greyhound named Tylenol, mostly because the shelter worker laughed when she said it, and because the dog was old and needed someone who wouldn't ask him to run.

On Tuesdays she still volunteers at the free clinic downtown, handing out pamphlets and making sure the waiting room has enough chairs. She doesn't tell people her nickname there. They just call her Lena.

Sometimes, late at night in her studio, she looks at herself in the mirror and wonders if she has ever actually arrived at that special state of being she was always aspiring to. She thinks maybe aspiration isn't a destination. Maybe it's a posture. Maybe it's just the act of staying soft enough that other people can rest against you for a while.

And if that makes your headache go away, even for an hour, then she figures the name was a good one. [end of text]


